1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
Winter has killed more humans than any war in history. Not any single winter.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:13,000
The cumulative toll across hundreds of thousands of years of human prehistory, across every season that turned cold

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:22,000
before anyone had central heating or insulated walls, or a supermarket that stayed open regardless of what the weather was doing outside, represents a

4
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:30,000
mortality pressure so consistent and so severe that it shaped human biology, human cognition, human social structure,

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:38,000
and human ingenuity in ways that are still visible today. And ancient humans didn't just survive winter. Some of

6
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:46,000
their solutions to it were so clever, so counterintuitive, and so far ahead of what you'd expect from people without written science or modern engineering

7
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:54,000
that they qualify by any reasonable standard as genuinely crazy in the best possible sense of the word. Let's go

8
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:02,000
through them because some of these will change how you think about the people who came before us. Start with the most counterintuitive one. The trick that sounds wrong until you understand the

9
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:10,000
physics and then sounds like obvious genius. Snow as insulation. Modern people think of snow as the enemy. It is

10
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:16,000
cold. It is wet. It is the substance that winter uses to make everything worse. The instinct when surrounded by

11
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:24,000
snow is to get away from it as fast as possible. Inside under blankets near a heat source. Ancient humans in cold

12
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:32,000
climates did the opposite. They built into the snow deliberately, enthusiastically, with a level of engineering sophistication that took

13
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:40,000
modern physicists to fully explain. The igloo is the most famous example. But the principle extends across dozens of

14
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:47,000
cold climate cultures that independently arrived at the same conclusion. Snow properly structured is one of the best

15
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:51,000
insulating materials available in a winter environment.

16
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:59,000
Packed snow has a thermal conductivity dramatically lower than still air. An igloo interior with a small group of

17
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:09,000
people and a single lamp burning animal fat can maintain temperatures 30 to 40° C warmer than the outside air. In an

18
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:17,000
Arctic winter where outside temperatures can drop to minus50, that difference is the difference between alive and dead.

19
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:28,000
The engineering of the igloo specifically is remarkable. The dome shape distributes stress across the structure, allowing it to support its own weight without internal supports.

20
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:35,000
The entrance tunnel is always lower than the sleeping platform inside. Cold air is denser than warm air and sinks. So,

21
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:43,000
the lower tunnel acts as a cold air trap, keeping the warmest air in the sleeping area. The blocks of snow are cut at a specific angle that allows them

22
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:51,000
to spiral upward into a self-supporting dome. A ventilation hole at the top prevents carbon dioxide buildup and

23
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:59,000
regulates temperature. The whole structure can be built by two experienced people in under two hours using nothing but a snow knife. This is

24
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:07,000
not primitive improvisation. This is applied physics arrived at through observation and refinement across generations, producing a solution that

25
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:15,000
modern engineers studying it for emergency shelter applications have been unable to meaningfully improve upon. And the Inuit were not the only people using

26
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:23,000
snow as insulation. Indigenous Siberian peoples dug into snow drifts to create sleeping cavities. Ancient Scandinavian

27
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:31,000
hunters used snow as windbreak material, packing it against the walls of temporary shelters to dramatically reduce heat loss. The principle was the

28
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:39,000
same everywhere cold. People looked at snow and realized it was not the enemy. Used correctly, snow was the solution.

29
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:47,000
Now, let's talk about a winter trick that sounds disgusting before it sounds brilliant. Fat everywhere. Not dietary

30
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:52,000
fat, though that too, which we'll get to. Physical fat applied to the body.

31
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:59,000
Ancient humans in cold climates from the Arctic to the frozen steps of ice age Europe applied animal fat directly to

32
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:07,000
exposed skin before going out in the cold. rendered bear fat, seal fat, caribou fat, whatever was available,

33
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:16,000
worked into the skin of the face, hands, and any other exposed surface before facing cold air and wind. This works.

34
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:24,000
The physics are straightforward. Fat is hydrophobic. It repels water, and winddriven moisture on skin dramatically

35
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:32,000
accelerates heat loss. A layer of fat on exposed skin acts as a physical barrier against that moisture contact, reducing

36
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:41,000
the effect of windchill experienced by the skin surface. It also reduces direct convective heat loss from the skin to moving air by creating a boundary layer.

37
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:49,000
Modern equivalents are everywhere in cold climate sports. The channel swimmer coating themselves in grease before entering the water. The Antarctic

38
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:57,000
expedition member applying protective balm before going outside. the winter ultramarathon competitor using petroleum jelly on exposed skin. The specific

39
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:03,000
material is different. The principle is identical to what ancient humans were doing tens of thousands of years ago.

40
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:12,000
But the fat application wasn't only about windproofing. In cultures that practiced it extensively, the Inuit, various Siberian peoples, some northern

41
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:21,000
European populations, the fat used often came from animals whose diets included specific plants or marine sources with high concentrations of fats soluble

42
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:28,000
vitamins, particularly vitamin D. In Arctic winters where sunlight was absent for months and dietary vitamin D from

43
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:35,000
food was critical, fat absorbed through skin contact may have provided meaningful supplemental nutrition.

44
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:46,000
Ancient humans applying seal fat to their skin before heading out into the Arctic dark may have been simultaneously protecting against windchill and getting vitamin D from an unusual source.

45
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:54,000
Whether this was understood consciously or simply practiced without understanding the mechanism doesn't change the fact that it worked. Let's

46
00:05:54,000 --> 00:06:02,000
talk about the clothing trick that seems obvious until you realize how counterintuitive its specific application was. Layering is the obvious

47
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:10,000
part. Multiple layers of clothing trap air between them, and trapped air is insulation. This principle is universal

48
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:18,000
across cold climate cultures and requires no explanation for modern people who use the same principle every time they dress for cold weather. The

49
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:25,000
counterintuitive part is how ancient cold climate clothing was designed to be adjustable, specifically to vent heat

50
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:29,000
during exertion and seal in warmth at rest.

51
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:37,000
The Inuit clothing system, the most sophisticated traditional cold weather garment design ever developed, included specific design features that allowed it

52
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:45,000
to be opened at the collar and cuffs during high exertion activities like running or heavy pulling to release body heat, then closed again when activity

53
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:52,000
slowed. The goal was to prevent sweating, not to maximize warmth. This sounds backwards. In the cold, you want

54
00:06:52,000 --> 00:07:01,000
to be warm, not to prevent yourself from getting warmer. But sweating in Arctic conditions is deadly. Sweat saturates the insulating layer closest to the

55
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:09,000
skin. Wet insulation loses most of its thermal value. And when exertion stops, which it always eventually does, a

56
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:17,000
person wearing wet insulating layers in minus40 temperatures has eliminated most of their protection at exactly the moment they need it most. Ancient cold

57
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:24,000
climate cultures understood this with a precision that modern people in central heating have largely lost. The management of body moisture was as

58
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:33,000
critical as the management of body temperature. The clothing systems they developed treated these as inseparable problems, designing garments that could

59
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:40,000
be modulated to keep the wearer dry as well as warm. Two separate anorak layers, an inner layer worn furs side in

60
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:48,000
against the skin, an outer layer worn furs side out against the wind, created a system where the air gap between them

61
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:57,000
could be regulated by how loosely or tightly the outer layer was worn. The specific design knowledge required to produce these garments, the pattern

62
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:05,000
cutting, the stitching techniques that made seams windproof without making them rigid, the selection and preparation of hides for different garment components

63
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:13,000
was complex enough that it took years to master and represented some of the most sophisticated technical knowledge in these cultures. A woman who could

64
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:25,000
produce a fully articulated Inuit winter suit was performing an engineering task of considerable complexity and the quality of her work had direct survival implications for the people wearing it.

65
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:33,000
Now let's talk about food tricks because winter food management in ancient cultures involved some solutions that were genuinely ahead of their apparent

66
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:40,000
technological level. Fermentation as winter preservation is the most interesting example. Traditional peoples

67
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:48,000
across the Arctic and subarctic Inuit up various Siberian groups practiced forms of food fermentation that produced

68
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:55,000
strongly flavored nutritionally dense preserved foods specifically for winter consumption. Fermented walrus, fermented

69
00:08:55,000 --> 00:09:02,000
fish heads, fermented seal flipper. The products of these preservation methods are famous in modern food culture

70
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:10,000
primarily for their extraordinary smell, which is a diplomatic way of saying that they are among the most intensely odoriferous foods ever produced by any

71
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:17,000
human culture in any period. But the nutrition they provided in the winter months was critical. Fermentation

72
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:26,000
preserves food without heat, making it viable in environments where fuel for cooking is scarce. It breaks down certain anti-nutritional factors in raw

73
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:34,000
animal products, improving the bioavailability of nutrients. In some cases, it produces vitamin C, a nutrient

74
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:46,000
absent from most Arctic animal foods in their raw state, but essential for preventing scurvy and produced as a byproduct of bacterial fermentation in certain traditional preparations.

75
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:54,000
Ancient [snorts] Arctic peoples were preventing scurvy through fermented foods in a period when the British Royal Navy, which had far more scientific

76
00:09:54,000 --> 00:10:01,000
resources available, was still losing sailors to it. By the way, if you want to go even deeper into the Secret Lives

77
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:10,000
of Ancient Humans, I just launched a full course called The Secret Lives of Ancient Humans. 10 modules, 3 plus hours

78
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:18,000
of content covering everything we never talk about on YouTube. what they feared, how they communicated, what hunted them,

79
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:23,000
and much more. Plus, an exclusive PDF guide included. Link in the description.

80
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:32,000
See you inside. Specific fermentation techniques were arrived at through generations of accumulated observation, not through understanding of vitamins or

81
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:39,000
bacterial metabolism. The knowledge was that the food prepared this way kept people healthy through winter in a way

82
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:47,000
that other preparations did not. The mechanism didn't need to be understood for the knowledge to be real and valuable. Now, let's talk about

83
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:54,000
something that ancient winter cultures figured out about food storage that goes beyond fermentation, and that represents a level of environmental engineering

84
00:10:54,000 --> 00:11:02,000
most people don't associate with prehistoric peoples. Selective caching, not just storing food for winter, that much is obvious, but storing specific

85
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:10,000
types of food in specific locations selected for their specific preservation properties. Based on accumulated knowledge of how different foods behaved

86
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:19,000
in different storage conditions over the winter period, ancient peoples in cold climates maintained multiple storage sites with different characteristics.

87
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:27,000
Perafrost caches preserved meat at consistently low temperatures. Elevated caches in trees or on raised platforms

88
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:34,000
protected food from ground level predators and kept it in conditions where freeze thaw cycles were minimized.

89
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:41,000
Some groups cached food in running water, cold, oxygenated, and at consistent temperature, which preserved

90
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:48,000
fish in ways that static storage didn't match. Different foods went to different cache locations based on empirically

91
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:57,000
developed knowledge of which storage conditions produced which preservation outcomes. This is not instinct. This is the product of paying attention across

92
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:04,000
seasons and years to what happened to stored food under different conditions, drawing conclusions, adjusting practice,

93
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:08,000
and transmitting the refined knowledge to the next generation.

94
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:17,000
It is a research program conducted over generations without a laboratory, arriving at solutions that matched the preservation physics of each food type

95
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:25,000
to the environmental conditions available. Fire management in winter deserves its own discussion because the tricks ancient humans developed for

96
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:34,000
maintaining and efficiently using fire in cold conditions were significantly more sophisticated than simply keeping a flame going. The position of a fire

97
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:43,000
relative to sleeping bodies is not obvious. Modern people think a fire in the middle of the space is the most effective. Heat all around. Ancient

98
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:51,000
peoples in cold climates generally understood something different. A fire positioned against a back wall with a reflector behind it, stones, packed

99
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:59,000
earth, bark, directs radiant heat outward toward the sleeping area much more efficiently than a central fire, which loses half its radiant heat to the

100
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:08,000
wall behind it. Archaeological evidence of fire placement in ancient shelters consistently shows the strategic positioning that maximizes radiant heat

101
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:13,000
delivery to sleeping areas, not simply central placement for social gathering.

102
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:24,000
Stone heating is another ancient technique that modern people encounter primarily in saunas and hot yoga studios without realizing they're using a technology thousands of years old.

103
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:33,000
Heating stones in a fire and then using them as heat sources is a fundamentally different approach to warmth than maintaining a continuous fire. Heated

104
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:42,000
stones can be placed inside sleeping areas where an open fire would be dangerous. They retain heat for extended periods. They can be reheated

105
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:49,000
periodically using a small fire rather than a large continuous one, reducing fuel consumption. And they can be used to warm water, which can then be used

106
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:56,000
for both drinking and as a warm compress against the body in hypothermic emergencies. Ancient peoples used heated

107
00:13:56,000 --> 00:14:03,000
stones for cooking, for heating sleeping spaces, for warming water, and for therapeutic heat application to cold

108
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:12,000
injured extremities. The technique required knowing which stones could be safely heated. Some stones, particularly those with trapped moisture or specific

109
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:20,000
mineral compositions, explode when rapidly heated, which is not the outcome you want in a confined winter shelter.

110
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:28,000
The knowledge of which stones were safe was practical, specific, and accumulated through experience across generations.

111
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:38,000
Let's talk about animal knowledge in winter because this is a category of ancient winter survival intelligence that deserves more attention than it typically receives.

112
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:46,000
Ancient humans in cold climates paid extremely close attention to animal behavior as a source of survival information. Animals in winter

113
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:54,000
environments have adapted to those environments over vastly longer periods than humans, and their behavioral solutions to winter problems represent

114
00:14:54,000 --> 00:15:03,000
optimized responses to the same challenges humans were facing. Watching where specific animals dend for winter told ancient humans something about the

115
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:10,000
best shelter sites, the locations with specific combinations of protection from wind, thermal mass from surrounding

116
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:17,000
earth or rock, and drainage that prevented accumulation of cold melt water. Animals choose these sites

117
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:21,000
through behavioral instincts refined over millions of years of selection.

118
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:31,000
Ancient humans who tracked those choices and used the same sites were essentially reading the environmental assessment of animals with far longer winter experience.

119
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:39,000
Migration pattern knowledge told ancient humans when food animals would be available and when they would be absent, allowing winter food storage to be

120
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:48,000
calibrated against the expected period of scarcity rather than simply accumulated until resources ran out. The timing and direction of animal

121
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:56,000
migrations carries information about coming weather. Animals that are moving when conditions suggest they shouldn't be often are responding to environmental

122
00:15:56,000 --> 00:16:06,000
cues below human sensory threshold. And ancient humans who watched for these behavioral anomalies had early warning of severe weather events.

123
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:15,000
The specific animals whose winter dens and behavioral patterns were most closely studied varied by region. bears in European and North American contexts,

124
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:22,000
various unullet across the Eurasian step, specific seal species in Arctic environments.

125
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:30,000
The principle was consistent. The animals already knew how to survive this winter. Watching them carefully was a form of field research conducted without

126
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:38,000
laboratories or hypotheses, but with life or death stakes attached to getting the conclusions right. Let's talk about caloric loading before winter because

127
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:45,000
the specific ways ancient peoples managed this process were more deliberate and more sophisticated than the general idea of eating a lot before

128
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:53,000
winter implies. Modern people experienced the autumn desire to eat more, to seek richer foods, to sleep

129
00:16:53,000 --> 00:17:01,000
more and move less as a combination of seasonal mood change and perhaps holiday food culture influence.

130
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:08,000
What it actually is biologically is an ancient behavioral program running as designed. The body responding to

131
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:18,000
decreasing light and temperature with a set of behaviors specifically intended to maximize fat accumulation before the cold season reduces food availability.

132
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:26,000
Ancient humans appear to have deliberately worked with this program rather than against it. In periods of autumn abundance, the posth harvest

133
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:34,000
period in agricultural cultures, the late summer hunting surplus in huntergatherer contexts, strategic overconumption of highfat, highc calorie

134
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:43,000
foods was culturally normalized and practically encouraged. The communal feasts of autumn and early winter in cultures across the northern hemisphere

135
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:52,000
are not simply celebrations. They are coordinated caloric loading events distributing surplus food through the community at the moment when surplus was

136
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:57,000
available and metabolic storage of that surplus was most advantageous.

137
00:17:57,000 --> 00:18:03,000
The specific foods chosen for these periods consistently reflect an understanding of caloric density. Animal

138
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:12,000
fats, marrow, rendered organ meats, fermented dairy in agricultural contexts, concentrated plant foods like

139
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:21,000
nuts and dried fruits. These are not coincidentally the most calorie dense foods available. They are the foods that most efficiently load the subcutaneous

140
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:30,000
fat reserves that would provide insulation and metabolic fuel through the cold months. The social structure of winter feasting served double duty. The

141
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:39,000
sharing of high calorie food ensured that even individuals who had been less successful in their own food acquisition entered winter with adequate reserves. A

142
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:47,000
group in which every member had sufficient fat stores going into winter lost fewer members to cold and starvation than a group where reserves

143
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:57,000
were unevenly distributed. The social norm of generous feast sharing was simultaneously a cooperative survival mechanism that improved group winter survival rates.

144
00:18:57,000 --> 00:19:06,000
There's one final category of ancient winter trick that ties everything else together and represents perhaps the most impressive cognitive achievement in the

145
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:10,000
entire list. Predictive calendar knowledge.

146
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:18,000
Ancient peoples across every northern culture developed sophisticated systems for tracking the progression of winter and predicting its turning points. Not

147
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:25,000
as abstract astronomical knowledge, but as survival critical operational information. Knowing when the winter

148
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:34,000
solstice had passed, when daylight was measurably increasing, when specific constellations indicated that the worst of winter was behind rather than ahead,

149
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:42,000
this knowledge affected decisions about how conservatively to ration remaining food stores, how much fuel to commit to heating versus preserving for longerterm

150
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:47,000
needs, whether to attempt winter travel or wait for conditions to improve.

151
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:56,000
Stonehenge, the medicine wheels of the North American plains, the solar alignments in Irish megalithic tombs like New Graange. These are often

152
00:19:56,000 --> 00:20:05,000
discussed in mystical or religious terms. They were also at a very practical level winter survival instruments. A structure that marks the

153
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:12,000
exact day of the winter solstice tells the people who built it that they have survived to the turning point and can adjust their resource management

154
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:21,000
accordingly. The most severe part of winter often comes after the solstice when fuel and food stores are already depleted. Knowing precisely where you

155
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:30,000
are in that progression is information that affects whether you survive to spring. But the calendar knowledge went beyond simply marking the solstice.

156
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:36,000
Ancient peoples tracked the entire arc of winter using multiple environmental indicators simultaneously.

157
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:43,000
star positions, the behavior of specific plant species, the depth and texture of frost, the behavior of animals

158
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:52,000
responding to lengthening days before humans can detectly perceive the difference. The ability to read these indicators and synthesize them into a

159
00:20:52,000 --> 00:21:00,000
confident assessment of where winter stood was a skill that took years to develop and that was held by the most experienced members of the community.

160
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:08,000
The person who could stand outside in the depths of February and say with confidence, "We are past the worst. The animals are already responding to the

161
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:17,000
change, we can loosen the food rationing slightly." Was providing the group with information that affected survival decisions in both directions. Rationing

162
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:21,000
too conservatively wasted food that could have maintained group strength.

163
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:28,000
Rationing too loosely risked running out before spring. The calendar knowledge that calibrated that decision was not

164
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:37,000
ornamental. It was operational. Ancient humans built observatories to predict winter with stone without writing.

165
00:21:37,000 --> 00:21:45,000
Before any civilization we would recognize by that name existed, the tricks were not crazy in the sense of being irrational.

166
00:21:45,000 --> 00:21:53,000
They were crazy in the sense of being far more sophisticated than the dye in a cave with a fur narrative gives them credit for. Snow as insulation. Fat as

167
00:21:53,000 --> 00:22:04,000
windproofing and vitamin supplementation. Moisture management in clothing as a survival priority equal to warmth management. Selective food caching matched to preservation physics.

168
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:13,000
Fermentation producing winter vitamins nobody knew existed. Strategic fireplacement based on radiant heat physics. Animal behavior as

169
00:22:13,000 --> 00:22:21,000
environmental monitoring and weather prediction. communal feasting as caloric loading and resource distribution. Stone

170
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:29,000
observatories as winter survival instruments calibrating rationing decisions that determined who made it to spring. Every one of these tricks was

171
00:22:29,000 --> 00:22:36,000
arrived at not through written science or formal research, but through the um accumulated observation of people who paid extremely close attention to

172
00:22:36,000 --> 00:22:46,000
everything because paying extremely close attention to everything was how they stayed alive. Winter has killed more humans than any war in history. But

173
00:22:46,000 --> 00:22:49,000
the humans it didn't kill left us the instructions.
